over the years we've seen plenty of  right-wing politicians sully whatever  credibility they had left trying to get  into the good graces of donald trump and  oftentimes it ends up just completely  backfiring or they get into trouble over  it and the next person to be following  this trend appears to be trump's fourth  and final white house chief of staff  mark meadows and that is because meadows  is no longer cooperating with the  january 6th congressional committee  meaning that if his abstinence continues  charges could indeed be coming his way  now as recently as a few weeks ago  meadows was cooperating with the january  6th committee he was providing them with  documents and really naming names like  the whole reason we know about the story  having to do with sean hannity and brian  kilmeade and laura ingram urging  meadows to tell trump to end the  insurrection was because meadows was  willing to hand over the documents he  was willing to hand over those text  messages to the committee obviously we  also found out that don jr was trying to  urge meadows to put an end to it and we  also found out that congressman jim  jordan was giving an outline to meadows  as to how mike pence could throw out the  electoral college votes in states that  they were contesting so this is all  based on the fact that meadows was  complying with the committee's requests  and handing over the documents but  everything changed around the time  meadows released his book the chiefs  chief and trump publicly lambasted  meadows for the claim specifically that  trump exposed people to covid after he  tested positive for the virus  and one of those people that he exposed  was actually joe biden during their  first debate trump called the allegation  fake news and meadows backtrack saying  oh yeah that part of my book that was  fake news apparently regretting that he  upset his former boss meadows thought  that the book was going to flatter trump  and instead it mainly just got under his  skin because of the media coverage  having to do with that positive test it  did the exact opposite of what he was  trying to do it was right after this  incident that meadows halted his  communications with the january 6th  committee the january 6th commission  wanted to get meadows to show up in  person for testimony uh meadow's lawyer  george terwilliger argued he had done  enough he had provided the documents and  the committee should be satisfied with  that in fact tara willager released a  statement that reads quote the select  committee's true intentions in dealing  with mr meadows have been revealed when  it accuses him of contempt citing the  very documents his cooperation has  produced so what mark meadow's attorney  is saying here is that basically you  should be grateful to my client because  so much of this information that you  have now is because he was willing to  cooperate with the investigation you  shouldn't be turning around now and  trying to rope him into testifying if he  doesn't want to he shouldn't be  threatening him with criminal charges  because you know he's given you so much  already and it is true that mark meadows  was fully cooperating and handed over uh  9 000 pages of documents even though  some of that information was indeed  damaging to meadows and to the former  president he still did do that but you  don't really get partial credit when  you're talking about a select committee  that has the rule of law behind them and  if you're not going to do everything  that they ask of you then you're going  to open yourself up to criminal exposure  and that's exactly what's happened here  recently the house voted to refer mark  meadows for criminal contempt of  congress charges and it's going to be up  to biden's justice department and  attorney general merrick garland to  decide whether or not he's actually  going to be charged and if he is and  he's found guilty there's actually the  possibility on the table of mark meadows  serving prison time over this now is  that actually going to happen i don't  know he may not even get charged in the  first place because it's going to be  seen as an overtly political act if this  happens like yes bannon was charged in  this investigation but he also wasn't  willing to comply at all  and bannon was in trump's administration  but only as an advisor whereas mark  meadows like he was the chief of staff  after all it's a much more important gig  and so maybe they would just figure that  biden and biden's justice department  wouldn't have the political capital to  charge mark meadows for his abstinence  in this case but we'll have to wait and  see and then obviously if he does get  charged that doesn't mean necessarily  he'll get convicted and if he does get  convicted that doesn't mean necessarily  that he'll go to prison over it he may  just face a fine or something like that  so we'll have to wait and see what  happens but you never know like  this could definitely happen and there's  definitely some legal exposure here and  meadows i'm sure is probably not  sleeping very well at night uh these  days and that's all because of donald  trump and he was trying to get on  trump's good side and now at the end of  the day he's not only on trump's bad  side uh he's also  running the risk of criminal charges so  that's mark meadows for you and now  there's this very very long list of  people who went down for donald trump's  sake you already thought it was long  enough well it's getting even longer we  have people on there like roger stone  steve bannon paul manafort michael flynn  rick gates george papadopoulos michael  cohen we know that rudy giuliani of  course is in trouble he's under  investigation and he's also facing that  civil suit with dominion voting systems  there's people like  mike lindell and sydney powell who are  also running against that case with  dominion voting systems so is mark  meadows the next to follow the next to  get in trouble trying to get into the  good graces of donald trump we'll just  have to wait and see but you'd think  that all these people would learn their  lesson by now  on the bonus show today we have some  great stories we're going to talk about  how kim potter the former police officer  she has been found guilty in  manslaughter charges having to do with  the the killing of dante wright kim  potter of course was the officer who  confused her service weapon with her  taser and she thought that she was  tasing dante wright but she actually  shot and killed him  she has been found guilty now so we'll  see what happens with sentencing we'll  see the reactions to all of that we're  going to talk about donald trump  declaring victory on the war on  christmas he talked to mike huckabee  about how before trump was president no  one would say merry christmas and you  weren't allowed to but ever since trump  served in office now all of a sudden you  can say merry christmas and i also want  to talk about a recent covet scare that  i had i think many of you will find that  story interesting if you want to watch  the bonus show today go to  joinpackman.com and sign up for a  membership or you can go to patreon.com  david pakman show and sign up for a five  dollar membership there that's going to  do it for today that's going to do it  for the week merry christmas to everyone  david will be back next week and we'll  see you then

